The current loop calls `bits.OnesCount64` once per word and pays the full loop overhead (phi, compare, increment, branch, index and bounds check) on every element. Each word's popcount is a fixed cost, so the cheapest thing to remove is per-element loop control. Unrolling by four with a slice-shrinking loop amortises that overhead across four words and leaves the per-word popcount calls unchanged, so correctness is preserved.

Caveat: I can't run giri, so the saving is an estimate. If giri lowers `OnesCount64` to a single instruction, the gain is still from loop overhead, which is the part I'm confident about.

